1. tx_customers_junction.csv: customer master data (5,100 rows)
Column	Findings
customer_id	CUST00001–CUST05100, unique, no gaps
customer_postal_code	5-character strings with leading zeros (00990). There are 290 distinct codes, all starting 00/01/02, so it's all Helsinki/Espoo/Vantaa. Top codes: 00940, 01300, 02230
customer_age	18–90, median 44. Heavy peak in the 40s (2,096 people). 56 people are 80+
Edge cases:

Read postal code as a string. Read as a number, 00100 becomes 100.
Every file starts with a UTF-8 BOM (byte-order mark), so open them with utf-8-sig. Otherwise the first column name gets a hidden prefix.
Only 200 of the 5,100 customers have a mortgage. Their ages run 32–77 (median 47).
2. mtg_tx_customer_link_and_final_mtg_infio.csv: final outcome per mortgage (200 rows)
Mapping: maps C0004…C1000 to CUSTxxxxx one-to-one. Every ID resolves in the customer file.
Decisions: 150 approved and 50 rejected. The rejection reasons are insufficient_collateral (34), insufficient_income (8), insufficient_equity (6) and existing_debt (2).
Ranges:
Property price: €156k–538k
Loan: €121k–528k
Equity: €10k–81.5k
Loan-to-value (LTV): 0.76–0.98
Required income: €2.2k–9.7k per month
Formulas I confirmed:
loan + equity = price for all 200 rows.
lfinal_loan_to_value = loan / price exactly. The column name has a typo (lfinal_).
required_monthly_income = loan × 0.0184086 for every row. That is exactly a 6% stress rate, 25-year annuity, divided by a 35% debt-to-income cap. This is how the bank tests affordability.
Odd points:
The final collateral value is below the loan in 198 of 200 cases, and 150 of those are still approved. The other two have collateral exactly equal to the loan.
The final collateral value counts only the primary apartment's valuation. The second "other_apartment" valuation is ignored in 114 cases (see below).
There is no clean threshold between approved and rejected. Approved LTVs run 0.76–0.98 and rejected ones 0.79–0.97. "Insufficient equity" cases cluster at LTV ≥ 0.91 with equity of roughly €13k–17.5k.
3. mtg_negotiation_history.csv: event log (3,452 rows, 200 customers)
Time: 2 Jan 2026 – 10 Sep 2026. Timestamps use the SAS format 26MAY26:00:00:00, which parses with %d%b%y:%H:%M:%S. Only 5 events fall in September, so the data looks cut off there.

Size per customer: 11–25 events (median 17), spanning 13–45 days (median 25). Rows are already sorted by time within each customer, and no customer has two events at the same timestamp.

The process is the same for every application: application_created (always at 00:00:00) → contacts / property_added / price updates → loan_calculation → income_information_received → expense_recorded → collateral_added → collateral_valuation → additional_collateral_requested → a second valuation → document_received → loan_decision → often a follow-up customer_contact.

Properties: 1–3 per customer (113 / 58 / 29 customers). The decision is always on the last property added. Earlier properties are abandoned, which is why prices in the history reach €647.5k while the final maximum is €538k. Price updates move the price −6% to +8%, and 85 of the 188 updates are decreases.

Valuations: the bank values the primary apartment at 65–80% of the price (median 72%). The second "other_apartment" is valued at €51k–243k.

Expenses: six types, each with a fixed set of amounts:

Type	Range
Bank fee	€100–350
Valuation fee	€250–450
Maintenance fee	€180–550 per month
Transfer tax	€2.5k–18k
Renovation	€5k–30k
Existing loan	€0–1,200
Existing loan is often exactly €0. Only 37 customers have an existing loan above zero, and 30 of them were still approved. A customer can record the same expense type twice; C0004 has two transfer_tax entries.

Notes: free text, but generated from only 21 templates. Every number in them is already in the structured columns, so the notes add no information.

Edge cases in the history:

Columns are sparse, and which ones are filled depends on the event type. For example, customer_contact rows sometimes carry property, loan or decision values copied forward and sometimes nothing.
decision is filled on 336 rows, not 200, because the contact after a decision repeats it (136 rows). Use only loan_decision rows for the outcome.
The last event is not always the decision: 136 of the 200 histories end with a customer_contact.
collateral_status reads approved even for applications that were rejected.
78 customers had extra collateral requested but never got a second valuation. 8 approved customers had no extra collateral request at all.
The final table matches the history exactly: same decision, reason, loan, equity and last price, and its collateral equals the primary valuation on the loan_decision row.